eeling of  speed like almost like a muscle car you  know so  yeah i really enjoyed working on the  a-wing the bombers are defined by being  a bit slower but heavier a bit tankier  built to drop that massive ordinance  that's going to take out big capital  ships things like star destroyers and  mc75s  so they might need a little support  getting there but once they get there  they can do an obscene amount of damage  for the bomber we wanted obviously to  make it look different than the  interceptor and fighter because it has a  very different  role and the exterior is also very  different so we needed to bring that  inside as well for the player  our job was made easier because the  bomber is a bigger ship  so especially when you're looking around  the interior you'll see there's actually  room where people can sit behind you  and all that's designed and looks very  different than the interior from the  other two ships  even if you look to the side you'll see  that there's there's a door  and there's the ordnance you know you  got all the bombs on the other side of  the compartment  so it makes it already feel quite  different just from that  you can actually go against the capital  ships with it you can make serious  damage  it needed to feel tankier kind of the  reverse approach  that we had for the interceptor for the  bomber we went and made  the frames of the viewport thicker more  armored kind of like you could ram  something and really take a lot of  punishment once again those secondary  screens that we introduced  we gave it a different shape for the  bomber something bulkier that just kind  of confirmed its role on the team  the ships that have the off center  cockpit like the bomber or the time  reaper were quite challenging because  as a player you don't want to feel like  all your control is offset by you know a  meter or two from from where the ship's  actually  located it becomes incredibly hard to  tell like can i get through that hole  i don't know where is the hole relative  to me so what we ended up doing is we  have the the  pilot's seat is technically off center  but the pivot point for the ship has  been moved relative to the the broader  shape of the ship  effectively you're driving it from the  center of the ship even though your seat  is a little offset what we do to make  the ships feel weighty is largely  playing with the tuning of their engines  so how fast they accelerate  how fast they decelerate and how much  momentum they preserve when you go into  a different maneuver if you're in a  bomber and you start to turn  the degree to which you have forward  momentum there helps sell that sense  that it's actually kind of a heavy ship  obviously the top end speed relative to  other ships  also helps that sense of weight and  there's a lot of kind of tricks of  presentation that help  it doesn't make a bit of difference in  the way the ship handles but in your  mind  that that sensation that it's a kind of  beefier ship helps make it  feel slower feel weightier similarly  with the sound design  when we're using a lot of lower pitches  that helps kind of correlate your mind  with the slower beefier stronger ship uh  than the more like high-pitched  tightly tuned interceptors so the y-wing  has been a really interesting challenge  for us because we're trying to capture  the the feel of the ship  and it's got a lot of similarities with  the x-wing in terms of its structure and  cockpit  but you still want it to feel  distinctive the cockpit team's done a  great job of looking at the  instrumentation looking at the  shape language that you see within that  dashboard of the film and then trying to  make use of that as well as possible  whether it's the kind of like tube  that works its way over the instruments  or the actual shapes you see of the  instruments there  we try to make use of those and really  lean into them so it feels right  for a y way at the same time kind of  that heavy structure you have around you  around kind of left and right behind you  you have a lot less of a window to your  astromech than you would in say an  x-wing  what's really interesting with the  y-wing is it's one of the the oldest  ships the rebels have  you can definitely see it in the design  so it was quite a challenge for us to  design something that would keep its  unique character but make it  playable because the ship is devoid of  screens for starters like the only one  that you see in the movies  is the targeting computer that comes in  from the side but besides that it  doesn't actually have eight displays  it's kind of like harkens back  to these sci-fi ships that had basically  blinking lights like these huge  computers right with like levers and  blinking lights you look at the wiring  the cabin for the pilot  it's been a versatile ship over  different time periods  and we've even seen it have two two  people in there  having the pilot and having like a rear  gunner so it's a very long cabin  and the pilot is kind of sitting with  like his legs out a bit more compared to  some of the other ships we had to play  you know with like the dimensions and do  a bunch of tricks to make it feel like  the shots especially  the ones you see in episode four but  they make it make it playable so one of  the things we did  was bring in displays that we designed  for the x-wing  and layered them with some of the iconic  things that were in the y-wing like the  tube and then that cone with the glass  lens that we used for the shift status  indicator  even on the screens i paid close  attention to the patterns that you have  on those  side panels like the painting patterns  and those big ribbed glass  lights that kind of stroke we took those  patterns and we made vector versions of  them and we used them as backing for the  information on the displays was  quite a challenge to be able to try and  keep that look  but introduce screens without uh you  know kind of disfiguring the y wing  the y wing for the longest time has been  our example of  how you make two very different builds  using components by default they're  built to be  bombers they're built to be really good  at taking down enemy capital ships  not as good at dog fighting depending on  the components you can slant them very  heavily toward  taking out capital ships and just accept  that you're going to be awful in a  dogfight and need the support of your  friends  or you can slant slanted more the other  way and be more of a heavy assault craft  where you're never going to be as zippy  as an a-wing and you know it's going to  be trouble if people get on your tail  but if they're dumb enough to come at  you from the front you can really end  their day in a hurry  what makes a ship part of the support  class is that you'll be piloting it to  help your friends and hinder your  enemies so you're gonna have a bunch of  tricks up your sleeve that  kind of give you the edge and slow down  your enemies  the thai reaper and the u-wing both of  our support ships actually come from the  film rogue one and we were really  excited to bring them to life in the  game because they haven't had as much  exposure yet  the reaper in particular just you gotta  love that design it's so cool i think  it's probably my favorite ship on the  imperial side  originally we looked at support and we  said okay we want kind of the entry  level class the class that's going to be  the easiest to use it can be helpful for  your team  but isn't as effective at killing did  not work uh for a couple of reasons  first is that the x-wing and the tie  fighter are what everybody knows  so no matter what we do those are going  to be the ships that anybody new or more  casual is going to gravitate toward  the other thing is we found that in a  dog fighter simply you know hunting down  enemies and shooting them  is the most basic thing you can do that  is the most successful and fundamental  part of gameplay  we ended up pivoting our mindset for  support ships  to be less the entry-level ship and  actually the other way around  they're more of a high-level ship that  takes a bit more skill to use  effectively  but they're the specialists you can use  a tie reaper  to sort of batten down the hatches  around your area to put up turrets drop  mines  to defend the zone you can do it to make  your entire team invisible to enemy  radar  to ionize enemy shields and bring them  down to  to uh you know focus fire they're very  versatile in terms of what they can do  with the widest array of different  components that can be equipped we were  bringing a ship to the screen  especially its cockpit that no one had  seen yet so the outside has been seen  but like this is going to be new to our  players there were some models that  existed because the ship  was designed for use for jedi fallen  order our biggest consideration  was uh visibility from the ship we kind  of cut the dashboard  down and made it shorter so the pilot  could sit closer to the front windows  they still look like what you expect  when you're seeing it from the outside  but once again you know through like  little little tricks  uh we make it you know more accessible  for players working on the u-wing has  been interesting because as you may know  it's a two-seater craft  and so trying to balance your  perspective in a way that feels right  that feels like you're in the  left-hand seat across the front there  but also pilots in a way you expect  where it's not difficult to fly through  a hole in a structure because oh  actually my center point is over there  has been really challenging  trying to make sure that our physics  lines up with kind of what your brain  expects as a pilot  especially in vr versus the actual  center point of the ship  the other big challenges as you may know  is that the u-wing has s-foils its wings  unfold  so when the u-wing is jumping into  hyperspace you've got the wings locked  and it's in its iconic u-shape  but once you're ready for combat they  fold back into more of a frankly a y  shape but don't tell the y  wings for combat and that means that  their their breath is quite a bit bigger  so if you're flying through say the  interior of a space station you've got  to be a lot more aware of your  surroundings than that you  than you might need to in something like  an away with both the the u-wing and the  reaper  they have places in them to have whether  it's a co-pilot or passengers in the  back  and if you look inside them in the game  you do see those seats but we don't  actually have them filled  now partially that's because our game is  set in space you're not landing on  planets that  you wouldn't have anywhere to drop them  off not a lot of reason for them to be  there fictionally  but also because from a performance  standpoint the way the game runs  we're trying to make sure we're giving  you the things that matter the most and  giving all the attention there  so the ships and lasers and explosions  are in front of you if we have a high  fidelity character  two feet away from you everything out  the window is gonna have to look worse  in order to make performance space for  that character right next to you  working on the u-wing was easier for two  reasons  one because it's one of the later ships  i tackled in production so we were  already getting  really used to finding creative  solutions to the channels we'd faced on  the other ships  also as luck would have it just the  design with that big central screen and  it has all that you know the mess of  wires and connectors and it has a  secondary monitor  on a swivel i was able to just take that  monitor move it  you know elsewhere where it fit with the  order that we have for components  because every ship is different  but gameplay-wise you know you want  people to to know that the radar is  going to be over on the left the speed  is going to be on the left because it  fits with the controller that kind of  stuff  so that let us easily move it around  without having to modify it as much so  what we did for the ewing is more  rescale and move around existing  elements and we were able to quickly  find a home  for uh for most of our gameplay  components i think for the most part i  only had to modify  the the top part of the central uh  console  so it would uh it would be able to  display our loadout and we used  something that's existing that you saw  like this kind of glass part with red  lights in the movie  it was close enough to our laser bar so  it was a really  a good fit so we've added these two new  ships  the thai defender and the b-wing we  designed the game around the eight core  playable ships  but fortunately thanks to all the  support of our players we're able to add  these entirely new ships  that fill new niches within the gameplay  and we think they're gonna add a lot to  the game  even though the ships are part of  existing classes they play very  differently they're quite unique and  they get their own unique components as  well  a big part of the design of squadrons is  about team composition and the way that  you kind of lay out your team and the  way that you support each other like  okay you know i'm going to need some  people that are handling just enemy  ships and i need some people that are  focusing on the objectives and so on  if we added a whole new class like we  called them the the heavy or something  that compositional picture got a bit  more complicated so instead we went how  do we fit these into the existing kind  of roles that we have  but offset enough in that kind of  spectrum of design  that they all feel unique we don't want  the the thai defender to feel like  it's a tie fighter with a different  cockpit you want it to feel distinctive  but we still want to understand the role  that okay  this is a flexible ship that can be used  both offensively and defensively  in the way that a tie fighter would i'm  a big fan of it from the thai fighter  days like the pc game tie fighter we  were so excited to see that come back  into canon and  okay well we gotta let people fly this  thing the reason we put it in that kind  of fighter class  is that it is meant to be flexible it's  something that can help offensively  defensively  where it's a bit different than say the  tie fighter or thai interceptor  is it's really good within a given area  it's not as good at getting from point  to point  heavily leans on boost and drift for all  your maneuverability  so you can really hold a spot but if you  need kind of superiority over a large  space to hold the front  you don't want to rely just on defenders  you're going to mix that up with a  different team composition  the tie defender being part of the same  you know line as the tie fighter bomber  or interceptor  obviously has to have a lot of things in  common with them  that's kind of the point with that  series for the pilots to just hop in  you know your home all the ships in the  series the main difference was  the viewports and also the secondary  monitors that we have on both sides of  that  central column that's standard in all of  them so the tie defender still has that  console in the middle and it has its own  set of handles on the side  one big thing with a tie defender is it  has shields  so it means it has more power management  going on and also has  more information that you need to be  able to consult as a player as you're  flying that then ship we had fun playing  with different shapes and patterns and  we found one that both felt robust  enough  to kind of feel like this is a ship that  can take punishment but also  still felt aggressive a bit closer to  the tie interceptor than the bomber  let's say  we want it to look like the empire spent  more money on this ship there's a little  bit more technology going on a little  bit more stuff  built in compared to the other three  core tie fighters the ship status  indicator if you look at the other  three basic ties besides the reaper what  we're showing you is the silhouette  of the the ship seen from the front  because the defender has shields  we wanted to show you a top-down view so  it would be clear right off the bat  you're looking at your front and rear  shields  i love its silhouette so aggressive with  all those those spikes  when you see from the top but it's also  a very challenging shape to work with  uh for the ui to try and you know like  surround it with the shields  and fit that nicely within the screen so  i spent  you know quite a bit of time doing  different iterations and patterns and  and line art to that would uh that would  really go well with the silhouette and  still stay clear for the player  in rebels the tie defender is just  straight up better than the other ties  in the context of a competitive game  that doesn't work great it's like all  right well you've got five ships take  that one it's completely superior  so what we've done lore wise is that  after thrawn's initiative uh you know  spoilers where things go sideways on  lothal bad defender  initiative is put out of service but the  plans are still around  so in our time period when the empire is  not doing so well  the plan still exists but they don't  have the resources they once did so  you're getting kind of a second  generation  more suited for mass production version  the whole game was designed to make you  feel like an ace pilot but the defender  is a whole other level  now i'm the elite within the ace pilot  group when i really master that ship  which is a great feeling  the cool thing about the b-wing is it's  the last ship from the return of the  jedi era that we wanted to bring to the  light  that we never got a chance to do with  the core game so the b-wing is really  cool because it's got this unique  gyroscopic cockpit you can actually keep  your cockpit stable  but control the entire rest of the ship  around the cockpit now partially this is  just  cool just for fun but also you can be  using this tactically so it's something  that improves your missile evasion it's  something you can use to aim your bombs  so you can have your ship like aiming  upward fly under a ship and shoot your  bomb straight up for example and beyond  that  it actually is increasing the rest of  your ammo so if you take that component  the one that lets you control your  gyroscopic cockpit  it's doubling whatever your other  auxiliary does so we were wondering  maybe  we would only allow you to have it  vertically or horizontal  and it would just kind of ease by itself  in between those motions once you  triggered it  or we could allow total control i'm  pretty pretty stoked that we're actually  able to allow you granular control so  you can actually place it at any angle  that you want as you're piloting we  decided to go kind of old school with a  dial that looks like some  something you'd have on an old fighter  plane and you actually have a needle  that's like a viewing silhouette shape  and you see it rotate around the dial so  you get like this precise feedback  you've got a bubble cockpit so if you're  doing free look or especially if you're  in vr  you could be just looking you know out  the window as that as that big wing is  moving along you'll see it like you know  block out the light  like across your dashboard you feel the  rumble if you're using a controller  you've got that kind of  shuttering metal scraping sound as the  entire ship moves around  you can be firing your lasers your beam  cannon or whatever while  you're rotating the wing as you see the  lasers kind of spiraling around your  cockpit  as that's rotating and you're still  firing this ship is ungainly you go  flying into a tight tunnel like in our  new map foster haven you got to really  be paying attention to where the  constraints of that ship are you smack  into a wall  and so having that that level of  situational awareness having the new  instrumentation once you actually  control and be aware of where the rest  of your ship is  uh is a whole new kind of experience  with the b-wing that's really unlike  anything else in the game  the references we had were mainly the  ships were built  for the original trilogy so you've never  seen them  in the movie like the inside but they  were built  like as part of that that ship they were  using for those outside shots as they  were flying through space  and then uh there was references from  the rubble's show for the prototype of  the b-wing from a fictional standpoint  the blade wing was the prototype so what  we're trying to capture is more of  the standard b-wing that comes along a  bit later and also because  there's just something cool about  feeling like when you saw return of the  jedi  that cockpit was actually there in that  b wing you just couldn't quite see it  because of the camera work  and we're finally getting to sort of  reveal that to the world you know very  much in first person  for the first time the b wing being one  of the later ships to be uh  introduced it's little subtle things  that will set it apart  making the screens a bit higher  resolution and playing you know with the  the type of crt or lcd monitors you're  seeing in some of the panels the beaming  has a mix  of like small crts for like its radar  and then the ship status indicator  but then it does have a nice big lcd  display for the combat screen closer to  what you'd see on the the u-wing  so the b-wing in lore is is a bomber you  know it's it's a bomber flash assault  craft but being  being a great bomber is a big part of  its identity so we wanted to keep that  concept  um but it's also such a unique ship you  know it's something that a lot of people  a lot of our fans have wanted to play  we've got a lot of feedback of the  b-wing where is it  okay we put it in guys gonna be okay  it's that idea of being able to control  your bombs  specifically that from a mechanical  standpoint fit well with the ship  we thought that was such a good fit that  it made sense to really lean into that  bomber role  the other thing is if you look at the  design of our ships the y-wing has  always been intended to be kind of a  spectrum  between going all bomber and more of a  dogfighter or somewhere in between we  give you quite a bit of flexibility  with the b-wing we've gone even further  to the right so it can dog fight but at  the most it's going to be a backline  fighter that's kind of providing  to give it like artillery support  whereas on the other extreme it is a  bomber  that is good for dropping bombs and  nothing else and you darn well better  escort it in terms of team composition  the thing with the b-wing  is that it is the farthest thing over in  the extreme of kind of the  i just murder people to i just murder  capital ships uh spectrum  of our different ships and so it's  something that doesn't work well on its  own this is a ship that benefits  tremendously from being paired with the  u-wing and to a lesser extent by being  paired with other ships that can support  and protect it  on its way to the desk to the  destination similarly you can set it up  to be more of a dog fighter  but even in that case it's not going to  be flying around in the middle of  everything the way that an a-wing would  it stays more at a distance and while  everyone else is keeping the enemy busy  it just guns them down you know  tremendous artillery so it's  very much a ship that leans into  teamwork you don't want to run b-wings  without the support of your buddies  [Music]  and that was every starfighter in star  wars squadrons  we hope this has helped you understand  the game a little bit better and brought  that galaxy far far away  a little bit closer to home
 

